;
CALLER PERMISSIONS AND PROGRAM APPROVAL

A - Deciding what callers may do
B - Approving programs for online use
C - Starting AUTH safely
D - The permission model and shipped defaults
E - Reading the map and querying targets
F - Setting drive and DU permissions
G - COM-file execution and F4
H - Troubleshooting

:A
DECIDING WHAT CALLERS MAY DO

An RCP/M gives strangers the use of a real computer. That's its attraction,
and also the reason for taking permissions seriously.

AUTH lives in SYSUTLS:. With the caller blanked, or between calls with BYE
attached, type AUTH /MAP. The map shows the effective read/write pair for each
DU. An asterisk marks an explicit override; without it the DU follows the
drive setting.

The shipped policy gives read access to A0:, I14:, J: and P:, read/write
access to I0:, and denies other areas. In particular, I13: and I15: inherit
I:'s denial. Don't casually grant access to all of I:; you would expose
inherited private areas unless you explicitly protect them first.

Suppose you want to keep J3: out of sight:

    AUTH J: /R
    AUTH J3: /NONE

To remove that exception, use AUTH J3: /INHERIT. A drive change doesn't erase
its DU overrides. Each permission argument replaces the entire pair: /W is
write only, not an instruction to add write permission to read.

AUTH changes take effect immediately and survive calls. Save the map with AUTH
/SAVE, accepting I13:AUTH.DAT, if you want it at the next startup. AUTH /LOAD
restores a saved map. The full reference in the appendix covers alternate
policies, scripts and recovery.

:B
APPROVING PROGRAMS FOR ONLINE USE

A disk full of useful CP/M programs is not necessarily a disk full of programs
suitable for remote execution. Debuggers, memory editors and arbitrary loaders
are obvious examples.

An ordinary caller COM load requires read access to its source DU, no caller
write access to that DU, and F4 on the actual file. F4 is our approval
convention, not a universal CP/M rule. For example, after reviewing Adventure
on the mounted GAMES disk:

    AUTH J1:ADV.COM
    AUTH J1:ADV.COM +X
    AUTH J1:ADV.COM

Withdraw approval with AUTH J1:ADV.COM -X. These changes go straight to the
disk directory; AUTH /SAVE and /LOAD affect permissions, not F4.

Clear inherited F4 flags when importing software. Review the actual binary and
its options, including shell escapes and secondary loaders. BYE is not a
sandbox for hostile Z80 code. See the caller execution policy and security
boundary for details. Approving a program means trusting it. Individual file
R/O and SYS attributes do not replace AUTH.

Don't give callers write access to A0: in an attempt to make a program work.
That will prevent ordinary public COM loads. If it happens, restore AUTH A0:
/R. A caller in writable WORK can still load approved commands from read-only
A0:.

:C
STARTING AUTH SAFELY

With a caller connected, use local Ctrl-B to blank the caller and enter
private sysop mode. Then use SYSUTLS:AUTH, or simply AUTH if the private
command path is active. Keep the caller blanked throughout administration.
Return to A0:SYSTEM before pressing Ctrl-B again to restore caller operation.

If administering between calls, local Ctrl-C opens BYE's menu. Select J to
jump to CP/M with BYE attached. AUTH needs that resident BYE interface; Return
for normal unpatched CP/M removes it and is not the AUTH environment.

AUTH rejects administration outside private sysop mode, even if someone copies
AUTH.COM to a public DU and marks it executable. These permissions control the
caller, not the private operator. Private mode permits maintenance on A:, I:,
J: and P: through the same policy bypass. Genuine host read-only media,
ordinary CP/M file R/O attributes and other file errors still apply.

Display help with:

    SYSUTLS:AUTH //

Commands and filenames are case-insensitive. AUTH accepts one target per
invocation; use several commands in a SUBMIT file or alias for a sequence.

:D
THE PERMISSION MODEL AND SHIPPED DEFAULTS

D: means a drive, for example J:. DU: means a drive plus user area, for
example J3:. Each drive has a caller permission pair. Each DU either inherits
that pair or has an explicit replacement. The order is:

    explicit DU setting -> drive setting -> default NONE

An explicit DU setting always takes precedence, including an explicit NONE. A
drive change preserves existing DU overrides. There are no automatic private
exceptions for particular drive letters: SYSUTLS and RBBS data are private
because of the configured policy, with a separate controlled RBBS service
grant.

The supplied startup policy grants:

    Scope                     Caller permission
    A0:SYSTEM                 R-  (public commands and banner)
    J: and P:                 R-  (public collections)
    I0:WORK                   RW  (temporary workspace)
    I14:HELP                  R-  (help topics)
    Other DUs                 --  (neither read nor write)

I13:SYSUTLS and I15:RBBS inherit I:'s NONE setting in that policy. If you
grant access to all of I:, those inherited DUs follow it too. To keep
particular DUs private under a broader drive grant, give them explicit NONE
overrides first. Keep the policy files and private board data protected
against caller writes.

R permits file reading and directory browsing. W permits file mutations
through available programs. Write-only settings deny reading/listing;
utilities that require read access for file lookup may not work with a
write-only DU. RW is permission, not a supplied file-transfer or copy service:
callers still have no general copy command or direct upload into WORK in this
package. The game launchers seed WORK, and caller programs can create
workspace data. UPLOAD continues to use the private host inbox described in
Chapter 12.

I0:WORK is cleared at startup and call recovery regardless of its AUTH
setting. Changing its permissions does not turn it into permanent storage.

:E
READING THE MAP AND QUERYING TARGETS

AUTH                      All drives, users 0-15
    AUTH /MAP                 Same complete map
    AUTH J:                   Only the J: row, users 0-15
    AUTH J3:                  Same complete J: row

These commands only display information. AUTH D: does NOT reset D: to NONE. No
permission change occurs without an explicit permission argument.

The map shows effective caller permissions for each DU. An asterisk before the
pair means that DU has an explicit override:

    *RW   Explicit read/write       *R-   Explicit read-only
    *-W   Explicit write-only       *--   Explicit denial
     R-   Read-only inherited from the drive (no asterisk)

A missing asterisk means inheritance, even when the inherited value is --. The
map does not show whether a disk is mounted or whether a particular file
exists. You can configure G3: even if no G: image is currently attached.

:F
SETTING DRIVE AND DU PERMISSIONS

Both slash syntax and map-style pairs are supported:

    Slash form       Map form       Also accepted       Result
    /RW              RW             RW                  Read and write
    /R               R-             R                   Read only
    /W               -W             W                   Write only
    /NONE            --             NONE                Neither

Every form REPLACES the complete pair. /W does not add W to an existing R; it
sets -W and removes read access. Likewise, /R removes write access. Do not
type the map's asterisk as part of a permission argument.

    AUTH J: /R                Set the drive's caller pair to R-
    AUTH J3: RW               Override J3: with read/write
    AUTH J3: /W               Replace that override with write-only
    AUTH J3: --               Explicitly deny both permissions on J3:
    AUTH J3: INHERIT           Remove its override; follow J: again

/INHERIT is equivalent to INHERIT and applies only to a DU. With J: set to R-,
J3: INHERIT becomes R- and loses its asterisk. Explicit J3: NONE remains
denied and keeps its asterisk even if the drive is readable. To deny a drive's
base permissions, use AUTH J: /NONE; that does not erase its explicit DU
overrides.

Changes take effect in BYE's resident map immediately and survive caller
hangups. They remain unsaved until you use /SAVE. They are replaced by /LOAD
or by the next startup's saved map.

:G
COM-FILE EXECUTION AND F4

Normal caller execution requires ALL of the following:

    - Read access to the executable's DU.
    - No caller write access to that DU.
    - F4 set on the actual COM file's directory entry.

The source DU matters, not the caller's current working directory. A caller in
writable I0: can run approved commands found on read-only A0:. The caller
cannot run a COM stored in writable I0:, even if it has F4 set. If you make
I0: caller-read-only, approved files there can run; they are still cleared at
call boundaries. Individual file R/O does not override the writable-DU ban.

All ordinary COM files use this rule, including public commands in A0:.
Setting A0: to RW therefore stops callers loading DIR, SDZ, LT and the other
COM commands there. Private sysop operation continues to work. To restore
normal caller commands, set AUTH A0: /R. See A.10.

Inspect, approve or revoke an existing exact filename:

    AUTH J1:ADV.COM            Report effective caller executable status
    AUTH J1:ADV.COM +X         Set F4; preserve other file attributes
    AUTH J1:ADV.COM -X         Clear F4; preserve other file attributes

The target comes first. AUTH +X J1:ADV.COM is not supported. These file
commands require a DU prefix, a COM extension and an exact name; wildcards are
rejected.

Examples of status output:

    J1:ADV.COM is executable.
    J1:ADV.COM is not executable: F4 is not set.
    J1:ADV.COM is not executable: DU is caller-writable.
    J1:ADV.COM is not executable: no caller read access.
    J1:ADV.COM not found.

+X is approval, not a way to override DU permissions. F4 is our
caller-approval convention, not a universal meaning imposed by CP/M. Clear
inherited F4 flags when importing software, then approve reviewed programs
deliberately. Individual file R/O is optional for execution and retains its
normal CP/M meaning. SET can also manage F4 in private mode. SYS controls
directory display, not approval.

F4 changes are written directly to the file directory. They do NOT require
AUTH /SAVE. Conversely, AUTH /LOAD does not undo any F4 changes. If a copied
file retains F4, it still cannot execute while its DU is caller-writable.

Approval means trusting the program, including external loaders, interpreters,
shell escapes and direct memory/disk operations it may provide. BYE is not a
sandbox for hostile Z80 code. The reviewed RBBS entry has its own narrow grant
for the private board executable/data; do not make those DUs public to run it.

:H
TROUBLESHOOTING

DIR?, SDZ? or LT? after making A0: RW:
    Caller-writable DUs cannot supply caller executables. Blank the caller and
    run AUTH A0: /R. Save the corrected map if the bad setting was saved.
    Query AUTH A0:DIR.COM if it still fails; also check that the file exists.

Game preparation failed:
    MOUNT GAMES first. I0: needs read/write access for the supplied launchers.
    Use AUTH I0: RW in private mode if necessary. The game executable's DU
    still needs caller read access, no write access and F4 approval.

I0: changed to -- after /INHERIT:
    It now follows I:'s NONE setting. Restore the explicit AUTH I0: RW
    override.

/LOAD did not undo an executable approval:
    Policy files hold DU permissions only. Use AUTH DU:FILE.COM -X to clear
    F4.

Changes disappeared after restart:
    Save to I13:AUTH.DAT, not just an alternate filename. Unsaved changes
    survive call boundaries but not a new BYE startup. Check that the save
    succeeded.

AUTH reports that private mode or an AUTH-enabled driver is required:
    Blank the caller locally. With no caller, use BYE's J menu choice to keep
    its resident interface attached. Do not use normal unpatched CP/M for
    AUTH.
    Check that the driver and utility came from a compatible package.

Explicit /SAVE says the file exists:
    Use /Y only if you intend to overwrite it, or choose a new filename.

Save reports a staging file already exists:
    Inspect the same-basename .$$$ before removing it. It may contain a
    complete
    recoverable map from a previous interrupted save; see A.9.

The optional host --caller-allow/deny/list helper is for stopped-image
maintenance. Normal live administration uses AUTH inside CP/M.
